  "summary": "Several major policy shifts are reshaping the healthcare landscape. The CDC's vaccine advisory committee has fundamentally refocused its charter to downplay vaccine recommendations and review alternatives, while Congress is moving on multiple fronts—with bipartisan support for expanded methadone access to treat opioid addiction and proposals to rein in the 340B drug discount program that hospitals rely on for funding. Meanwhile, political control over federal health agencies is tightening under the Trump administration, including a proposed new CDC science office designed to exert closer oversight.\n\nIn artificial intelligence, the field is maturing beyond automation toward genuine transformation. The FDA has granted breakthrough designations to multiple generative AI devices, including two for interpreting chest X-rays and drafting radiology reports, while well-funded startups like xCures and Assort Health are raising tens of millions to expand AI clinical data platforms and patient journey agents. However, a persistent challenge remains: most healthcare AI implementations fail after pilot phases, suggesting execution gaps despite strong funding and regulatory momentum.\n\nDrug pricing and biotech competition are intensifying scrutiny. Democrats and the public are demanding answers about exclusive access to Eli Lilly's experimental obesity drug, while lawmakers are increasingly focused on Chinese biotech competition despite the Biosecure Act already in place. A Supreme Court ruling blocking thousands of lawsuits against Roundup's maker signals significant limits on product liability litigation in health-related cases.",
